        content:
          "<p><strong>March to May</strong> and <strong>October to December</strong> both work well. Spring brings rhododendron flowering along the climb from Sikles to Tasha, which is spectacular on this particular stretch of forest, and warm days on the ridge. Autumn and early winter give the crispest views of Annapurna II and IV, which is what most people come for.</p><p>Because the route tops out below 3,900 m it stays walkable later into the winter than most Annapurna trails, though the Kori ridge is exposed and can hold snow from January. The monsoon is the season to avoid: the forest section is leech-heavy, the ridge is usually in cloud, and the road to Sikles is prone to landslides.</p>",
